  "narrative": "The enrichment analysis is complete. I have confirmed pathway memberships and verified the key upstream/downstream relationships. I am ready to write the narrative.\n\n---\n\n**Pathway Analysis Narrative**\n\nThe metabolomics dataset reveals two strongly enriched pathways with non-overlapping driver compounds.\n\n**Eicosanoid Synthesis (WikiPathways WP167)** is the top candidate (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 1251). Five metabolites belong to this pathway: **arachidonic acid (C00219)**, **leukotriene A4 (C00909)**, **15-deoxy-Δ12,14-PGJ2 (C14717)**, **5-HETE (C04805)**, and **15-HETE (C04742)**. Arachidonic acid sits at the apex of this network. KEGG path-finding confirms it is a direct precursor of leukotriene A4 (2-hop path: C00219 → C05356 → C00909) and is also upstream of 5-HETE (forward path: C00219 → C05356 → C04805). These compounds collectively span the lipoxygenase and cyclooxygenase branches of eicosanoid metabolism, implicating intense phospholipase-driven arachidonate release and downstream enzymatic processing.\n\n**Selenium Micronutrient Network (WikiPathways WP15)** is the second major pathway (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 365). It is driven by the cysteine–cystathionine axis: **L-cysteine (C00097)** and **L-cystathionine (C02291)** together with the same arachidonate derivatives. L-Cysteine is confirmed upstream of L-cystathionine via a 3-hop bidirectional KEGG path (C00097 → C00022 → C00065 → C02291), consistent with transsulfuration.\n\n**Mercaptopurine (C02380)** occupies a distinct pharmacological niche, mapping to the Mercaptopurine Metabolism Pathway and Azathioprine Action Pathway (SMPDB). It shares no direct KEGG reaction link with the eicosanoid cluster, suggesting it represents a parallel perturbation — potentially a treatment-side artefact or a purine-analogue intervention modulating the same inflammatory axis.\n\n**Biological significance.** Co-enrichment of 5-HETE and 15-HETE alongside leukotriene A4 implies concurrent activation of the 5-lipoxygenase (5-LOX) and 12/15-LOX branches, which drive neutrophil chemotaxis and the resolution of inflammation, respectively. The presence of 15-deoxy-PGJ2, a cyclopentenone prostaglandin with electrophilic anti-inflammatory properties, further suggests a counter-regulatory feedback attempt. Simultaneously, L-cystathionine elevation reflects heightened transsulfuration flux, supporting de novo glutathione synthesis — a hallmark of oxidative-stress response during inflammatory activation. The convergence of lipid mediator and sulfur-amino-acid signals points to a treatment-elicited inflammatory/anti-inflammatory cascade with concurrent redox burden.",
